' --- manual: everything below this line is hand-authored; the script preserves it ---
' The automated pass reads data members and little else. It drops every
' method, it drops a static or using member, it drops a declaration that
' wraps onto a second line, and a facet of free functions does not arrive at
' all -- so a repository, a service, a handler, a registrar and a history
' provider all arrive as empty boxes. The blocks below fill those boxes and
' draw the relationships the automated pass cannot see.
'
' They use fully-qualified class names rather than a reopened namespace
' block, because this PlantUML version crashes when a namespace that already
' holds classes is declared a second time. Redeclaring a class merges its
' members into the box the automated pass already drew, so a refresh keeps
' both.

' ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
' Domain value types
' ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

class ores::iam::domain::token_settings <<struct>> {
    +access_lifetime_s : int
    +party_selection_lifetime_s : int
    +max_session_s : int
    +refresh_threshold_pct : int
    {static} +load(svc : system_settings_service&) : token_settings
}
note right of "ores::iam::domain::token_settings"
The token-lifetime policy, loaded once per handler construction from
ores.variability's system_settings_service. The four members are the
iam.token.* settings: access_lifetime_seconds,
party_selection_lifetime_seconds, max_session_seconds and
refresh_threshold_pct. The defaults in the header (1800, 300, 28800,
80) apply only until load() overwrites them, so the settings table is
the single place the policy lives.
end note

class ores::iam::messaging::split_principal {
    {static} +split_principal(principal : string) : principal_parts
}
class ores::iam::messaging::username_of {
    {static} +username_of(principal : string) : string
}
note right of "ores::iam::messaging::split_principal"
Free functions, hand-written in principal.hpp. A principal is
"username@hostname": the username is the account key a login looks up,
the hostname routes the request to the tenant that serves it. The split
is on the last '@', so a username may itself contain one. The rule lives
here because call sites once each wrote it out, and the ones that forgot
it stored an account under "name@host" while login looked up "name".
end note

note right of "ores::iam::messaging::principal_parts"
The two parts a principal decomposes into, plus has_hostname so a caller
that routes by hostname knows whether one was stated. A bare username
routes nowhere.
end note

' ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
' Repositories. account_repository is the reference instance and is stated
' in full. Every generated repository carries the same contract: sql(), the
' write overloads under a claim, the read_latest family, read_all and
' read_at_version for a versioned table, a total-count read, and the three
' remove forms. The claim is what makes a write safe: replace_claim states
' the version the caller read (must_match_version), or that no row exists
' (must_not_exist), and the store decides in the write's own transaction. A
' row that moved on is a conflict, never a silent overwrite. remove(id,
' version) applies the same rule and answers removed, conflicting, missing
' or unsupported, so a caller reports an outcome instead of catching.
' ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

