}
note right of "ores::iam::service::internal_impersonation_service"
Mints short-lived JWTs for server-side actor impersonation. The token
carries the same jwt_claims shape the real login path produces --
including the account's effective permissions and visible-party set --
but with no password check, because the server never holds a plaintext
password. It is deliberately not exposed on any NATS subject; the
visible-party resolver is injected as a function so the service stays
unit-testable without a live NATS connection. A default TTL of 60
seconds makes each token use-once and discardable.
end note

class ores::iam::service::internal_request_client {
    +internal_request_client(nats : client&, token : string, refresh_token : function<string()>)
    +request(req : Request, timeout : seconds) : Request::response_type
    +wait_for_workflow_instance(instance_id : string, timeout : seconds, expected_steps : size_t, on_progress : function<void(const string&)>) : workflow_wait_result
}
note right of "ores::iam::service::internal_request_client"
Drives the real NATS handler pipeline as an impersonated client. It
attaches the token as a plain Authorization header (not
X-Delegated-Authorization: this request originates an identity, it does
not forward one) and throws rather than returning a result type, because
a server-side orchestrator has no user to show a graceful error to. On a
token_expired rejection it re-mints through the refresh callback and
retries once -- needed because wait_for_workflow_instance polls for
minutes and outlives the token's TTL.
end note

note bottom of "ores::iam::service::internal_request_client"
Thrown when the server rejected the request at the protocol level (an
X-Error reply header) is the nested service_error, as distinct from the
std::runtime_error thrown on transport or parse failure. It carries the
error_code so a caller can tell "the server told us why, and retrying
will not help" apart from a transient hiccup worth retrying. The nested
workflow_wait_result carries the exact failure reason of a workflow wait
so an orchestrator can surface it verbatim.
end note

